# Language Detection


This project has two components: a speech-to-text transcription utility and a text-based language classifier. They are demonstrated separately in this notebook and are not currently integrated into a single end-to-end pipeline.


## 1. Speech-to-Text Transcription


A utility function that transcribes an audio file to text using Google's speech recognition API, trying multiple language models (German, French, Japanese, Hindi) in sequence until one successfully recognizes the speech.


```python
transcribe_audio("German.wav")
```


Returns the transcribed text and reports which language model matched.


## 2. Text-Based Language Classification


A supervised ML pipeline that classifies short text samples into one of three languages (German, French, Italian).


Pipeline:
- Data cleaning (symbol/number removal, lowercasing, URL stripping)
- Feature extraction via Bag-of-Words CountVectorizer)
- Label encoding
- Train/test split (80/20)


Models compared:

| Model | Accuracy |
|---|---|
| K-Nearest Neighbors | 83.2% |
| Random Forest | 97.9% |
| Multinomial Naive Bayes | — |


Random Forest was the best-performing model, with precision/recall/F1 all at or above 0.95 across all three language classes (see classification report in notebook).


## Known Limitation


The speech-to-text component and the text classifier are not currently connected — the classifier is trained and evaluated on pre-existing labeled text data Language Detection.csv), not on audio transcription output. Integrating them (transcribe audio → classify language of transcript) would be a natural next step.


## Requirements


```
speechrecognition
googletrans
